Let $\Omega_t\subseteq\mathbb R^2$ be open, let $\beta\in\mathbb C$, $\omega>0$, and let $\tilde F,\tilde G:\Omega_t\to\mathbb C^3$ be $C^1$ maps (componentwise, in the sense of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). Write $\theta(x_3,t):=\beta x_3-\omega t$ and define $F:\Omega_t\times\mathbb R\times\mathbb R\to\mathbb R^3$ by $F(x_1,x_2,x_3,t):=\operatorname{Re}\bigl(\tilde F(x_1,x_2)e^{i\theta(x_3,t)}\bigr)$ componentwise, and $G$ likewise from $\tilde G$; here $e^{i\theta}$ is the complex exponential of [[def-complex-exponential]] and $\operatorname{Re}$ the real part of [[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]. Then $F$ is $C^1$ and, for every point of the domain:
(a) $\partial_{x_j}F=\operatorname{Re}\bigl(\partial_{x_j}\tilde F\,e^{i\theta}\bigr)$ for $j=1,2$, $\partial_{x_3}F=\operatorname{Re}\bigl(i\beta\tilde F e^{i\theta}\bigr)$, $\partial_tF=\operatorname{Re}\bigl(-i\omega\tilde F e^{i\theta}\bigr)$, where the divergence and curl are those of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]] and the partial derivatives are those of [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]];
(b) $\operatorname{div}F=\operatorname{Re}\bigl((\partial_1\tilde F_1+\partial_2\tilde F_2+i\beta\tilde F_3)e^{i\theta}\bigr)$;
(c) $\operatorname{curl}F=\operatorname{Re}\bigl((\partial_2\tilde F_3-i\beta\tilde F_2,\; i\beta\tilde F_1-\partial_1\tilde F_3,\; \partial_1\tilde F_2-\partial_2\tilde F_1)e^{i\theta}\bigr)$;
(d) [amplitude injectivity] if $C\in\mathbb C^3$ and $\operatorname{Re}\bigl(Ce^{i\varphi_1}\bigr)=\operatorname{Re}\bigl(Ce^{i\varphi_2}\bigr)=0$ for two real phases with $\varphi_1-\varphi_2\notin\pi\mathbb Z$, then $C=0$; consequently, if $F=G$ on a nonempty open subset $U$ of $\Omega_t\times\mathbb R\times\mathbb R$, then $\tilde F=\tilde G$ on the projection of $U$ to $\Omega_t$ (a nonempty open subset of $\Omega_t$).

Let $L_1,L_2>0$ and $R:=(0,L_1)\times(0,L_2)$. For integers $m,n\ge0$ put $h_{mn}(x_1,x_2):=\cos\bigl(\frac{m\pi x_1}{L_1}\bigr)\cos\bigl(\frac{n\pi x_2}{L_2}\bigr)$ and $k_{mn}^2:=\bigl(\frac{m\pi}{L_1}\bigr)^2+\bigl(\frac{n\pi}{L_2}\bigr)^2$; for integers $m,n\ge1$ put $e_{mn}(x_1,x_2):=\sin\bigl(\frac{m\pi x_1}{L_1}\bigr)\sin\bigl(\frac{n\pi x_2}{L_2}\bigr)$. Then, with $\Delta=\partial_1^2+\partial_2^2$, $\nabla=(\partial_1,\partial_2)$ and the Riemann integrals over the rectangle of [[def-multidimensional-rectangle-and-volume]]:
(a) $\Delta h_{mn}=-k_{mn}^2h_{mn}$ on $R$ and $\partial_1h_{mn}=0$ on both faces $x_1\in\{0,L_1\}$, $\partial_2h_{mn}=0$ on both faces $x_2\in\{0,L_2\}$; hence $\partial_nh_{mn}=0$ on every open face of $\partial R$;
(b) $\Delta e_{mn}=-k_{mn}^2e_{mn}$ on $R$ and $e_{mn}=0$ on $\partial R$ for $m,n\ge1$;
(c) [orthogonality and norms] $\int_Rh_{mn}h_{m'n'}=0$ unless $(m,n)=(m',n')$, and $\int_Rh_{mn}^2=\frac{L_1L_2}{4}(1+\delta_{m0})(1+\delta_{n0})$; $\int_Re_{mn}e_{m'n'}=0$ unless $(m,n)=(m',n')$, and $\int_Re_{mn}^2=\frac{L_1L_2}{4}$ for $m,n\ge1$; the mixed integrals are instead $$\int_Rh_{mn}e_{pq}=J_{mp}(L_1)J_{nq}(L_2),\qquad J_{mp}(L):=\int_0^L\cos(m\pi x/L)\sin(p\pi x/L)\,dx,$$ for $m,n\ge0$, $p,q\ge1$. Here $J_{mp}(L)=0$ when $m,p$ have matching parity (including $p=m$), and $J_{mp}(L)=2pL/[\pi(p^2-m^2)]$ when their parities differ. In particular $\int_Rh_{00}e_{11}=4L_1L_2/\pi^2$, so the sine and cosine systems are not mutually orthogonal;
(d) [Dirichlet-type identities] $\int_R|\nabla h_{mn}|^2=k_{mn}^2\int_Rh_{mn}^2$ for all $m,n\ge0$ and $\int_R|\nabla e_{mn}|^2=k_{mn}^2\int_Re_{mn}^2$ for $m,n\ge1$.

In the setting of [[pthm-em-rectangular-mode-candidates]] let a TE or TM candidate of part (a) or (b) with $\beta>0$ and $\omega>\omega_c$ be fixed (so $\beta=\sqrt{\omega^2\varepsilon\mu-k_c^2}$ is real and nonzero), retain its positive longitudinal amplitude $H_*$ (A m$^{-1}$) in the TE case or $E_*$ (V m$^{-1}$) in the TM case, let $T:=2\pi/\omega$, and let $u:=\frac12(E\cdot D+B\cdot H)$ and $S:=E\times H$ be the stored-energy density and energy-flux vector of [[pthm-em-restricted-material-energy]], with the Euclidean inner and cross products of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] and [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]. Define the period-averaged stored energy per unit length and the period-averaged longitudinal flux across the cross-section $$U:=\frac1T\int_0^T\!\!\int_{R_{ab}}u\,dx_1dx_2\,dt,\qquad \Phi:=\frac1T\int_0^T\!\!\int_{R_{ab}}S\cdot e_z\,dx_1dx_2\,dt,$$ both independent of $z$ and of the starting time. Then:
(a) [Cross-section identities] $\int_{R_{ab}}|\nabla_th_{mn}|^2=k_c^2\int_{R_{ab}}h_{mn}^2$ and $\int_{R_{ab}}|\nabla_te_{mn}|^2=k_c^2\int_{R_{ab}}e_{mn}^2$, with the boundary terms of the integration by parts vanishing;
(b) [Energy and flux] for the TE candidate $$U=H_*^2\frac{\mu^2\varepsilon\omega^2}{2k_c^2}\int_{R_{ab}}h_{mn}^2,\qquad \Phi=H_*^2\frac{\omega\mu\beta}{2k_c^2}\int_{R_{ab}}h_{mn}^2,$$ and for the TM candidate $$U=E_*^2\frac{\varepsilon^2\mu\omega^2}{2k_c^2}\int_{R_{ab}}e_{mn}^2,\qquad \Phi=E_*^2\frac{\omega\varepsilon\beta}{2k_c^2}\int_{R_{ab}}e_{mn}^2,$$ with the explicit norms of [[lem-em-rectangle-mode-profile-identities]](c);
(c) [Energy transport and speed relations] $\Phi=U\,\beta/(\varepsilon\mu\omega)=U\,v_g$, where $v_g:=d\omega/d\beta=\beta/(\varepsilon\mu\omega)$ is the group speed of the branch $\omega(\beta)=\sqrt{(\beta^2+k_c^2)/(\varepsilon\mu)}$; moreover the phase speed $v_p:=\omega/\beta$ satisfies $v_pv_g=1/(\varepsilon\mu)$, $v_p>1/\sqrt{\varepsilon\mu}$ and $0<v_g<1/\sqrt{\varepsilon\mu}$ for the declared indices;
(d) [Finite propagating set] if a candidate with indices $(m,n)$ is propagating at frequency $\omega$ (that is, $\beta^2=\omega^2\varepsilon\mu-k_{mn}^2>0$), then $m<\omega a\sqrt{\varepsilon\mu}/\pi$ and $n<\omega b\sqrt{\varepsilon\mu}/\pi$, so the propagating indices lie in the explicitly bounded index box $\{m,n\ge0: m<\omega a\sqrt{\varepsilon\mu}/\pi,\, n<\omega b\sqrt{\varepsilon\mu}/\pi\}$;
(e) [Scope] This is a lossless separated-guide computation: the period average is over time at fixed position, the flux is through one cross-section, $v_p$ is not asserted to be a signal speed and no bound on information or energy speeds in general dispersive media follows; the material branch and the PEC model are those of the cited theorems.

